course: dictionary
lesson: 11861
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The word bones can mean the hard parts inside your body, the pieces left in meat or fish, or the main outline of something. Context tells you which meaning the writer wants. «The X-ray showed that my arm bones were not broken.» Here bones are the hard parts inside your body.
explain: «Be careful of the fish bones when you eat your tea.» The words fish and eat show that the bones are the small pieces inside the fish, not the bones inside a person. Ask yourself: which meaning fits the words around it?
explain: «The bare bones of the story were a boy, a dog and a lost map.» Bare bones here means the main parts, with the extra details left out. Try the swap test: if you put the body meaning into that sentence, it makes no sense, so you know to choose the other meaning.
fact: Bones is the plural of bone, so one bone and many bones.
fact: An adult has 206 bones. A baby has about 300, and many of them join together as the child grows.
fact: Bones support your body, protect your organs (the skull protects the brain, and the ribs protect the heart and lungs), and work with muscles to move you.
fact: Calcium from milk and exercise help keep your bones strong.
fact: Bone can also be a verb, meaning to take the bones out of fish or meat.
exercise: «Be careful of the fish bones when you eat.» What do bones mean here? | The scales on a fish's skin | Pieces of wood from a boat | Long, thin noodles | *The small hard pieces inside fish
exercise: «The bare bones of the story were a boy, a dog and a lost map.» What do bare bones mean here? | A story set in a graveyard | *The main parts, with the extra details left out | Bones that have no skin on them | A story about a skeleton
exercise: Which of these is true about bones? | *An adult has 206 bones | Bones are made of wood | A baby has fewer bones than an adult | Bones are soft, like jelly
test: «Please pick out the bones before you give the chicken to the dog.» What does bones mean here? | Parts of the dog's tail | Loud, hard noises | Long sticks in the garden | *Pieces of bone from the chicken
test: What helps keep bones strong? | *Calcium from milk and exercise | Doing nothing at all | Sweets and sitting still | Sleeping in the sun
exam: Mum boiled the chicken bones to make soup. Which meaning of bones is this? | *Pieces of bone from an animal, left in the meat | Wooden poles for a tent | The hard parts inside a person's body | The main outline of a story
